# Steward — Lessons learned (AGENT 1)

Append-only log of what this domain learned the expensive way. The filename is
**`LESSONS-LEARNED.md`** — exactly this, in every project; a fork in this name silently splits a
domain's memory in two.

**The bar — when a lesson is owed.** An incident earns an entry when it **cost real time or produced
a wrong claim**. Not "something happened". Not a restatement of a rule that already exists — link
that one instead. A near-twin of an existing lesson supersedes it; it does not sit beside it.

**Taxonomy.** `observation` (first sighting) → `candidate_rule` (2nd independent occurrence) →
`accepted_rule` (recurred across actors, or the user confirmed it) → `deprecated`. Only an
`accepted_rule` binds this domain without re-asking.

**If a lesson is universal** — it would be true on any project, not just this one — it does not
belong here in the long run: it is a **proposal to change the method itself**. Write it here with
its evidence, then raise it; the method is amended deliberately, versioned, and migrated in. A
lesson parked as "universal" in a domain log is a rule that never became one.

**Every `accepted_rule` names its ENFORCEMENT TIER** — and if it is tier 3, says why no detector
exists. This is the field that keeps this file from becoming the place where good rules go to be
ignored: *the method's worst governance failure was a lesson that had been written and left at
tier 3 — writing it down a second time would have changed nothing.*
Tiers: **1 = impossible** (the violation cannot be expressed) · **2 = detected deterministically** (a
script finds it whether or not anyone remembered) · **3 = written down and hoped for**.

**Rotation:** the same aging process as `DECISIONS.md` (`contract-memory § 2`) — mark, then move to
`archive/LESSONS-LEARNED-archive-YYYY-MM.md`, one file per month of the entry's date, on either
trigger, and leave one pointer line per rotated id in the list below, in the one shape the
controls read (`/crit-doc-lint [25]`); the atlas keeps every archived id resolving. The pointer
list stays where it is: a pointer is never an entry and never rotates.

```
    - `L-A1-###` — ROTATED YYYY-MM-DD to archive/LESSONS-LEARNED-archive-YYYY-MM.md (done | superseded by <id> | consolidated in <id>)
```

**Rotated (the pointers):**

**The shape of an entry** — copy it under the rule below, fill it:

```
    ## L-A1-### (observation | candidate_rule | accepted_rule, the date) — the lesson as a one-line title
    **What happened.**  the incident, concretely: what was done, what broke, how it was found. 1–2 lines.
    **The rule.**       one imperative line — what to do instead, next time. If it does not fit in one
                        line, you have an observation, not a rule.
    **Amended YYYY-MM-DD (`<id>`):**  (later, optional) what changed, naming the entry that changed it — a
                        dated line here, never a second heading under this id
    **Enforcement:**    tier N — the detector that enforces it; or, for tier 3, why none is possible.
                        One written form, read by `/crit-doc-lint [11]`: the bold label, `tier N`, an em dash.
    **Bound:**          (optional) the contract-session § 6 bound this instantiates — TIME · LAYER · SHAPE ·
                        TARGET · INSTRUMENT · ACTION · ABSENCE · BASELINE — when the lesson confirms one
                        rather than stating new law.
    **Why it generalizes** (optional; fill it when promoting) — what makes this true beyond this incident.
    **Carries.**        one list line per id — this lesson's own at least. The generated IN-FORCE.md collects
                        every binds: line; its tier comes from the line, else from Enforcement above:
                        - `L-A1-###` · <the rule, one imperative line> · binds: <lane>[ · tier N[ — <detector>]]
                        - `L-A1-###` · <the rule> · settled: done | superseded by <id> | consolidated in <id>[ — <why>]
```

